  "N11" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-case: OLA1/YchF binds to both fully assembled ribosomes and individual subunits Source: (19,0,0)"];
  "N12" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Proteins binding to both assembled ribosomes and subunits typically regulate ribosome assembly/disassembly Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N13" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: OLA1/YchF likely regulates ribosome assembly/disassembly dynamics Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N14" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Currently, limited research exists on D/E-rich mRNA translation Source: (32,0,0)"];
  "N15" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If research is limited on a specific mRNA type, investigating translation factors for that type addresses a knowledge gap Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N16" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: D/E-rich mRNA translation mechanisms need investigation Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N18" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If ABCF proteins handle poly-acidic sequences, then other factors may handle D/E-rich (acidic) sequences Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N20" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-case: OLA1/YchF regulates translation on subset of mRNAs (leaderless mRNAs) Source: (14,0,0)"];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: Translation factors regulating specific mRNA subsets typically have sequence/structure specificity Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: OLA1/YchF likely has specificity for certain mRNA sequences/structures Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N23" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, ribosome rescue requires splitting stalled ribosomes Source: (26,0,0)"];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If ribosomes are split during rescue, then preventing premature re-association ensures proper rescue completion Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N25" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Factors preventing ribosome re-association are needed for rescue mechanisms Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: OLA1/YchF is involved in stress response pathways Source: (7,0,0)"];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If a protein is involved in stress responses, it likely participates in quality control mechanisms like ribosome rescue Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N28" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: OLA1/YchF participates in ribosome quality control/rescue Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N31a" [label="(0,0,0) Induction-common: If ribosomes stimulate ATPase activity, then the ATPase likely functions in ribosome-related processes Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N31b" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: YchF ATPase activity is functionally coupled to ribosome interactions Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N32a" [label="(0,0,0) Reference: 70S ribosome stimulates ATPase activity of YchF Source: (26,13,0)"];
  "N34" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If OLA1/YchF is an ATPase stimulated by ribosomes, then it likely uses ATP hydrolysis for ribosome-related functions Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N37a" [label="(0,0,0) Reference: ABCF proteins handle poly-acidic sequences Source: (32,0,0)"];
  "N5" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-case: Currently, lack of structural data capturing OLA1/YchF-ribosome complex Source: (23,0,0)"];
  "N6" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If structural data is lacking, then determining precise binding sites requires new structural analysis Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N7" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Need for structural determination of OLA1/YchF binding site on ribosome Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "N8" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: OLA1/YchF binds to ribosomes across species (E.coli, yeast, Trypanosoma, plants, humans) Source: (18,0,0)"];
  "N9" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-knowledge: If a protein binds to ribosomes universally, it likely has a conserved functional role in translation Source: (0,0,0)"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper-level aim is to investigate OLA1/YchF as a conserved ribosome-associated ATPase whose ribosome binding, structural binding site, effects on split-ribosome re-association, and potential mRNA-subset specificity should be developed and evaluated to explain its role in translation, including D/E-rich mRNA translation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When structural gaps, conserved ribosome binding, ATPase coupling, ribosome rescue logic, and mRNA-subset specificity all point to the same factor, they jointly motivate a focused study of how that factor regulates translation through ribosome-associated mechanisms."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The absence of structural data for an OLA1/YchF-ribosome complex motivates new structural analysis to determine the precise ribosomal binding site of OLA1/YchF."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Cross-species ribosome binding by OLA1/YchF supports the hypothesis that OLA1/YchF has a conserved ribosome-associated role in translation."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Given that preventing premature ribosome re-association is important for rescue completion and that OLA1/YchF is implicated in ribosome quality-control or rescue, OLA1/YchF is a plausible candidate to test for effects on split-ribosome re-association."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The limited understanding of D/E-rich mRNA translation, together with evidence that OLA1/YchF may act on specific mRNA subsets, motivates testing whether OLA1/YchF influences translation of D/E-rich mRNAs."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Because YchF ATPase activity is functionally coupled to ribosome interactions, OLA1/YchF likely uses ATP hydrolysis in ribosome-related functions."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) Reported stimulation of YchF ATPase activity by the 70S ribosome supports linking YchF ATPase activity to ribosome-related processes."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because ABCF proteins are reported to handle poly-acidic sequences, it is plausible that additional translation-related factors may also be relevant to D/E-rich acidic sequence translation, but this motivates investigation rather than proving a requirement."];
